<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lanao sultanates</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1904 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of Maranao Muslim principalities around Lake Lanao in Mindanao that resisted Spanish and American conquest for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranao-people">Maranao people</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lanao">Lake Lanao</a> plateau from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao Sultanate</a> and the Sulu coast in the 16th and 17th centuries. Local datus adopted the title of sultan and grouped their communities into the Pat a Pangampong ko Ranao, the four principalities of Bayabao, Masiu, Unayan and Baloi, which shared customary law and lineage ties.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Authority was widely dispersed: dozens of sultans and datus ruled villages, balanced by councils, genealogy and Islamic law alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a>. No single ruler commanded the whole confederation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranao-people">Maranao</a> were farmers, metalworkers and weavers, known for brass work, the okir carving style and the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darangen">Darangen</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Spanish expeditions, notably that of 1639 and the campaign of 1891 to 1895, failed to subdue the lake. After 1898 the Americans fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranao-people">Maranao</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bayan">Bayang</a> in 1902 and in later campaigns, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a> under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moro-province">Moro Province</a> by 1904. Royal houses still survive with customary authority.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lanao-sultanates.jpg" alt="Lanao sultanates" width="320" height="316"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ConfederateStatesOfLanao1616.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1904 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maranao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pat a Pangampong ko Ranao, Confederate States of Lanao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1752&amp;focus=lanao-sultanates" aria-label="Lanao sultanates on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8040 1758 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lanao sultanates lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8379 2176h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8360 2014h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8360 2014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Philippines. <a href="/atlas?year=1752&amp;focus=lanao-sultanates">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
